-- British Nationality Act 1981, s 1(5)-(5A), on two time axes.
--
-- Companion to "Multi-Temporal Rule Modeling". The GST example beside this
-- one moves ONE axis at a time. This file exists because a real provision
-- moves two at once, and the fragment below is the clearest small case of it
-- in the corpus.
--
-- THE TWO IDEAS. An adoption is effected on a day. The law that governs it
-- was itself substituted and amended on days of its own. Both dates bear on
-- one evaluation, and they are NOT the same idea:
--
--   * WHICH FACTS?  Was the adopter a British citizen THEN? Habitually
--     resident THEN? Section 1(5A) says this outright -- the requirements
--     must be met "on the date on which the order is made or the Convention
--     adoption is effected". That is the VALID-TIME axis, and the facts live
--     in the ledger.
--
--   * WHICH TEXT?   Section 1(5) and (5A) in their present form were
--     SUBSTITUTED with effect from 1 June 2003, and limb (b) was AMENDED
--     with effect from 30 December 2005, when the words "or in a designated
--     territory" were inserted. That is the RULE-EFFECTIVE axis.
--
-- THE SECTION FIXES THE FIRST AND SAYS NOTHING ABOUT THE SECOND. An earlier
-- version of this file tied both axes to the day of the order and called
-- that what s 1(5A) says. It is not. "On the date on which the order is
-- made" is an instruction about facts. Which version of the TEXT to apply is
-- a separate question, governed by commencement and by the presumption
-- against retrospectivity, and it is decided outside this section.
--
-- The question is live, and Meng put it (2026-09-15): a child adopted before
-- 30 December 2005 by adopters habitually resident in a designated territory
-- fails limb (b) on the text then in force. After the amendment, does the
-- same adoption now satisfy limb (b) -- making the child a citizen "as from
-- the date on which the order is made", which is to say retrospectively?
--
--   PROSPECTIVE READING: the status crystallises at the order, under the
--   text then in force. The 2004 adoption fails, permanently.
--
--   RETROSPECTIVE READING: s 1(5) is a standing provision. Ask today whether
--   "the requirements of subsection (5A) are met" on the historic facts,
--   using today's text; if they are, the child "shall ... be a British
--   citizen as from the date on which the order is made" -- backdated.
--
-- Both readings are encoded below as separate functions, and Case 9 is the
-- scenario on which they disagree. This file does NOT decide between them.
-- What is on record, and what is not:
--
--   * F16's savings, "(with Sch. 4 paras. 6-8)", do not settle it: those
--     paragraphs are transitional provisions about pending applications for
--     freeing orders, freeing orders, and pending applications for adoption
--     orders, not about the temporal reach of s 1(5A).
--   * The best argument for the prospective reading is a pattern rather than
--     an authority on this section. Where Parliament has wanted a historic
--     nationality exclusion fixed, it has legislated an express registration
--     route -- s 4C, inserted in 2003 for those born before 1983 to British
--     mothers, requires an application and registers the person going
--     forward. If an amendment backdated status by itself, that machinery
--     would be unnecessary.
--   * That is an argument from the shape of the Act, not a decision on this
--     provision, and no case on the point was read. It is exactly the call
--     the project's review gate reserves to a lawyer.
--
-- SCOPE. An illustration of the axes, not a replacement for the corpus
-- encoding at jl4/examples/legal/bna/bna.l4, which models all of section 1
-- and takes its facts as a GIVEN record instead.

IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

TIMEZONE IS "Etc/UTC"

§ `The statutory days`

-- F13: s 1(5) and (5A) substituted by the Adoption (Intercountry Aspects)
-- Act 1999, in force 1 June 2003.
`the substitution of s 1(5) and (5A)` MEANS Date 1 6 2003

-- F16: "or in a designated territory" inserted in s 1(5A)(b) by the Adoption
-- and Children Act 2002, in force 30 December 2005.
`the designated-territory amendment` MEANS Date 30 12 2005

§ `The facts, as they stood on the day`

-- Each fact is a ledger cell. A write under EVAL UNDER VALID TIME carries a
-- valid-from stamp; a read under EVAL UNDER VALID TIME selects the entry
-- whose interval covers that day. Before the first entry the answer is a
-- principled NOTHING, which `fromMaybe FALSE` turns into "not shown".
`the adopter a British citizen` MEANS
    fromMaybe FALSE (RECALL `the adopter is a British citizen`)

`the second adopter a British citizen` MEANS
    fromMaybe FALSE (RECALL `the second adopter is a British citizen`)

`the adopter habitually resident in the United Kingdom` MEANS
    fromMaybe FALSE (RECALL `the adopter is habitually resident in the United Kingdom`)

`the adopter habitually resident in a designated territory` MEANS
    fromMaybe FALSE (RECALL `the adopter is habitually resident in a designated territory`)

`the second adopter habitually resident in the United Kingdom` MEANS
    fromMaybe FALSE (RECALL `the second adopter is habitually resident in the United Kingdom`)

`the second adopter habitually resident in a designated territory` MEANS
    fromMaybe FALSE (RECALL `the second adopter is habitually resident in a designated territory`)

`a joint adoption` MEANS
    fromMaybe FALSE (RECALL `the adoption is a joint adoption`)

`a Convention adoption` MEANS
    fromMaybe FALSE (RECALL `the adoption is a Convention adoption effected under the law of a country or territory outside the United Kingdom`)

§ `The text, as it stood on the day`

-- The only moving part in this fragment: before 30 December 2005 limb (b)
-- read "habitually resident in the United Kingdom" and nothing else.
`the designated-territory words are in force` MEANS
    (DATE_SERIAL `RULES EFFECTIVE DATE`) AT LEAST (DATE_SERIAL `the designated-territory amendment`)

`the section as substituted is in force` MEANS
    (DATE_SERIAL `RULES EFFECTIVE DATE`) AT LEAST (DATE_SERIAL `the substitution of s 1(5) and (5A)`)

GIVEN `resident in the United Kingdom` IS A BOOLEAN
      `resident in a designated territory` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`habitual residence qualifies` `resident in the United Kingdom` `resident in a designated territory` MEANS
        `resident in the United Kingdom`
    OR  (`the designated-territory words are in force` AND `resident in a designated territory`)

§ `The requirements`

@ref British Nationality Act 1981 s 1(5A)(a)
`1(5A)(a) — the adopter, or one of the adopters, a British citizen` MEANS
        "(a) the adopter or, in the case of a joint adoption, one of the adopters is a British citizen"
    ... `the adopter a British citizen`
        OR  (`a joint adoption` AND `the second adopter a British citizen`)

@ref British Nationality Act 1981 s 1(5A)(b)
`1(5A)(b) — habitual residence, in a Convention case` MEANS
        "(b) in a case within subsection (5)(b), the adopter or, in the case of a joint adoption, both of the adopters are habitually resident in the United Kingdom [F16 or in a designated territory]"
    ... IF NOT `a Convention adoption`
        THEN TRUE
        ELSE (`habitual residence qualifies`
                  `the adopter habitually resident in the United Kingdom`
                  `the adopter habitually resident in a designated territory`)
             AND (IF NOT `a joint adoption`
                  THEN TRUE
                  ELSE `habitual residence qualifies`
                           `the second adopter habitually resident in the United Kingdom`
                           `the second adopter habitually resident in a designated territory`)

@ref British Nationality Act 1981 s 1(5A)
`1(5A) — the requirements are met` MEANS
        "(5A) Those requirements are that on the date on which the order is made or the Convention adoption is effected (as the case may be)—"
    ... `1(5A)(a) — the adopter, or one of the adopters, a British citizen`
    AND `1(5A)(b) — habitual residence, in a Convention case`

§ `The two readings`

-- The general form. The facts are always read at the day of the order --
-- that much s 1(5A) settles. The version of the TEXT is a second, free
-- parameter, and choosing it is the interpretive act.
GIVEN `the day of the order` IS A DATE
      `the version of the text` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the requirements are met, on the facts at` `the day of the order` `under the text at` `the version of the text` MEANS
    `EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` `the day of the order`
        (`EVAL UNDER RULES EFFECTIVE AT` `the version of the text` `1(5A) — the requirements are met`)

-- PROSPECTIVE READING: the text in force on the day of the order.
GIVEN d IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the requirements are met, on the text in force on the day of the order` d MEANS
    `the requirements are met, on the facts at` d `under the text at` d

-- RETROSPECTIVE READING: the facts of the day of the order, against the text
-- in force when the question is asked.
GIVEN `the day of the question` IS A DATE
      `the day of the order` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the requirements are met, asked on` `the day of the question` `about an order of` `the day of the order` MEANS
    `the requirements are met, on the facts at` `the day of the order` `under the text at` `the day of the question`

§ `Cases`

-- One directive is one ledger, so each case writes its own facts and then
-- asks its own question. Writes come first and evaluate left to right; the
-- last element of each LIST is the answer.
--
-- Every write is stamped with the day the fact BEGAN to hold, not the day
-- anyone wrote it down.

-- CASE 1. Sole adopter, a British citizen since 1995. UK court order, 2004.
-- Not a Convention adoption, so limb (b) is vacuous. Requirements met.
-- expected: LIST TRUE, FALSE, FALSE, TRUE
#EVAL LIST
    ( `EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (Date 12 3 1995) (RECORD `the adopter is a British citizen` IS TRUE) ),
    ( `EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (Date 1 1 1983) (RECORD `the adoption is a joint adoption` IS FALSE) ),
    ( `EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (Date 1 1 1983) (RECORD `the adoption is a Convention adoption effected under the law of a country or territory outside the United Kingdom` IS FALSE) ),
    ( `the requirements are met, on the text in force on the day of the order` (Date 1 6 2004) )

-- CASE 2. The order came FIRST. The adopter naturalised on 1 September 2006,
-- two years AFTER the 2004 order. Section 1(5A) asks about the date of the
-- order, so the requirements were NOT met -- even though the adopter is a
-- British citizen now. The fourth element is the same fact read with no
-- valid time in scope: last write wins, TRUE. That gap between the third and
-- fourth answers is the whole argument for the axis.
-- expected: LIST TRUE, FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, TRUE
#EVAL LIST
    ( `EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (Date 1 9 2006) (RECORD `the adopter is a British citizen` IS TRUE) ),
    ( `EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (Date 1 1 1983) (RECORD `the adoption is a joint adoption` IS FALSE) ),
    ( `EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (Date 1 1 1983) (RECORD `the adoption is a Convention adoption effected under the law of a country or territory outside the United Kingdom` IS FALSE) ),
    ( `the requirements are met, on the text in force on the day of the order` (Date 1 6 2004) ),
    ( `the adopter a British citizen` )

-- CASE 3. Naturalised ON the day of the order. "On the date on which the
-- order is made" includes that date, and a valid-time interval starts
-- inclusively, so the requirements are met.
-- expected: LIST TRUE, FALSE, FALSE, TRUE
#EVAL LIST
    ( `EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (Date 1 6 2004) (RECORD `the adopter is a British citizen` IS TRUE) ),
    ( `EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (Date 1 1 1983) (RECORD `the adoption is a joint adoption` IS FALSE) ),
    ( `EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (Date 1 1 1983) (RECORD `the adoption is a Convention adoption effected under the law of a country or territory outside the United Kingdom` IS FALSE) ),
    ( `the requirements are met, on the text in force on the day of the order` (Date 1 6 2004) )

-- CASE 4. Joint adoption; the first adopter is not a British citizen, the
-- second is. Limb (a) wants "one of the adopters", so it is satisfied.
-- expected: LIST FALSE, TRUE, TRUE, FALSE, TRUE
#EVAL LIST
    ( `EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (Date 1 1 1983) (RECORD `the adopter is a British citizen` IS FALSE) ),
    ( `EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (Date 4 7 1999) (RECORD `the second adopter is a British citizen` IS TRUE) ),
    ( `EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (Date 1 1 1983) (RECORD `the adoption is a joint adoption` IS TRUE) ),
    ( `EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (Date 1 1 1983) (RECORD `the adoption is a Convention adoption effected under the law of a country or territory outside the United Kingdom` IS FALSE) ),
    ( `the requirements are met, on the text in force on the day of the order` (Date 1 6 2004) )

-- CASE 5. Convention adoption effected 1 June 2004. Sole adopter, a British
-- citizen, habitually resident in a designated territory and NOT in the
-- United Kingdom. On 1 June 2004 limb (b) still read "habitually resident in
-- the United Kingdom" and stopped there -- the designated-territory words
-- were not inserted until 30 December 2005. Requirements NOT met.
-- expected: LIST TRUE, FALSE, TRUE, FALSE, TRUE, FALSE
#EVAL LIST
    ( `EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (Date 12 3 1995) (RECORD `the adopter is a British citizen` IS TRUE) ),
    ( `EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (Date 1 1 1983) (RECORD `the adopter is habitually resident in the United Kingdom` IS FALSE) ),
    ( `EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (Date 1 1 1990) (RECORD `the adopter is habitually resident in a designated territory` IS TRUE) ),
    ( `EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (Date 1 1 1983) (RECORD `the adoption is a joint adoption` IS FALSE) ),
    ( `EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (Date 1 1 1983) (RECORD `the adoption is a Convention adoption effected under the law of a country or territory outside the United Kingdom` IS TRUE) ),
    ( `the requirements are met, on the text in force on the day of the order` (Date 1 6 2004) )

-- CASE 6. Case 5's facts, on Case 5's day, read against the text as it stood
-- on 1 June 2006. Nothing about the adoption has changed; only the words
-- have. This is the counterfactual the statute cannot ask and the two axes
-- can, and it isolates what F16 did.
-- expected: LIST TRUE, FALSE, TRUE, FALSE, TRUE, FALSE, TRUE
#EVAL LIST
    ( `EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (Date 12 3 1995) (RECORD `the adopter is a British citizen` IS TRUE) ),
    ( `EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (Date 1 1 1983) (RECORD `the adopter is habitually resident in the United Kingdom` IS FALSE) ),
    ( `EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (Date 1 1 1990) (RECORD `the adopter is habitually resident in a designated territory` IS TRUE) ),
    ( `EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (Date 1 1 1983) (RECORD `the adoption is a joint adoption` IS FALSE) ),
    ( `EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (Date 1 1 1983) (RECORD `the adoption is a Convention adoption effected under the law of a country or territory outside the United Kingdom` IS TRUE) ),
    ( `the requirements are met, on the facts at` (Date 1 6 2004) `under the text at` (Date 1 6 2004) ),
    ( `the requirements are met, on the facts at` (Date 1 6 2004) `under the text at` (Date 1 6 2006) )

-- CASE 7. Joint Convention adoption, 2006. One adopter habitually resident
-- in the United Kingdom, the other in neither. Limb (b) wants BOTH of the
-- adopters, so it fails, and the designated-territory words do not help.
-- expected: LIST TRUE, TRUE, FALSE, FALSE, TRUE, TRUE, FALSE
#EVAL LIST
    ( `EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (Date 12 3 1995) (RECORD `the adopter is a British citizen` IS TRUE) ),
    ( `EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (Date 1 1 1990) (RECORD `the adopter is habitually resident in the United Kingdom` IS TRUE) ),
    ( `EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (Date 1 1 1983) (RECORD `the second adopter is habitually resident in the United Kingdom` IS FALSE) ),
    ( `EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (Date 1 1 1983) (RECORD `the second adopter is habitually resident in a designated territory` IS FALSE) ),
    ( `EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (Date 1 1 1983) (RECORD `the adoption is a joint adoption` IS TRUE) ),
    ( `EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (Date 1 1 1983) (RECORD `the adoption is a Convention adoption effected under the law of a country or territory outside the United Kingdom` IS TRUE) ),
    ( `the requirements are met, on the text in force on the day of the order` (Date 1 6 2006) )

-- CASE 8. Citizenship held and then lost. The adopter was a British citizen
-- from 1995 and renounced in 2002; the order is 2004. The later interval
-- covers the day of the order, so the requirements are not met. A single
-- Boolean field cannot express this at all.
-- expected: LIST TRUE, FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, TRUE
#EVAL LIST
    ( `EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (Date 12 3 1995) (RECORD `the adopter is a British citizen` IS TRUE) ),
    ( `EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (Date 30 9 2002) (RECORD `the adopter is a British citizen` IS FALSE) ),
    ( `EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (Date 1 1 1983) (RECORD `the adoption is a joint adoption` IS FALSE) ),
    ( `EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (Date 1 1 1983) (RECORD `the adoption is a Convention adoption effected under the law of a country or territory outside the United Kingdom` IS FALSE) ),
    ( `the requirements are met, on the text in force on the day of the order` (Date 1 6 2004) ),
    ( `the requirements are met, on the text in force on the day of the order` (Date 1 6 1999) )

-- CASE 9. THE CASE THE TWO READINGS DECIDE DIFFERENTLY (Meng, 2026-09-15).
--
-- A Convention adoption effected 1 June 2004. Both adopters are habitually
-- resident in a designated territory and in no part of the United Kingdom.
-- On the text in force that day, limb (b) required residence in the United
-- Kingdom and stopped there, so the requirements were not met and the child
-- did not become a British citizen.
--
-- Then on 30 December 2005 the words "or in a designated territory" are
-- inserted. Nothing about the adoption changes. Ask the question again in
-- 2006:
--
--   element 8  -- prospective: the 2004 text still governs the 2004 order.
--                 Not met. The child never became a citizen.
--   element 9  -- asked in 2005, before the amendment. Not met on any
--                 reading; this is the control.
--   element 10 -- retrospective: the 2004 facts against the 2006 text. Met
--                 -- and s 1(5) then says the child is a citizen "as from
--                 the date on which the order is made", i.e. from 2004,
--                 two years before anyone could have known it.
--
-- The axes do not decide which is right. They make the disagreement visible
-- and force the choice to be stated, which is the argument for keeping them
-- apart. See the header for what is and is not on record.
-- expected: LIST TRUE, FALSE, TRUE, FALSE, TRUE, TRUE, TRUE, FALSE, FALSE, TRUE
#EVAL LIST
    ( `EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (Date 12 3 1995) (RECORD `the adopter is a British citizen` IS TRUE) ),
    ( `EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (Date 1 1 1983) (RECORD `the adopter is habitually resident in the United Kingdom` IS FALSE) ),
    ( `EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (Date 1 1 1990) (RECORD `the adopter is habitually resident in a designated territory` IS TRUE) ),
    ( `EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (Date 1 1 1983) (RECORD `the second adopter is habitually resident in the United Kingdom` IS FALSE) ),
    ( `EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (Date 1 1 1990) (RECORD `the second adopter is habitually resident in a designated territory` IS TRUE) ),
    ( `EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (Date 1 1 1983) (RECORD `the adoption is a joint adoption` IS TRUE) ),
    ( `EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (Date 1 1 1983) (RECORD `the adoption is a Convention adoption effected under the law of a country or territory outside the United Kingdom` IS TRUE) ),
    ( `the requirements are met, on the text in force on the day of the order` (Date 1 6 2004) ),
    ( `the requirements are met, asked on` (Date 1 6 2005) `about an order of` (Date 1 6 2004) ),
    ( `the requirements are met, asked on` (Date 1 6 2006) `about an order of` (Date 1 6 2004) )
